The amendment above reported two things about the pod's return and implied one event. The address half is
right. The weights half is right about TODAY and wrong about WHEN, and the correction is worth far more than
the error: the swap happened a month before this campaign began, so it is not news about the maintenance, it
is news about every number above it.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, read first-hand from the pod's own engine configuration (`/etc/zds-inference/engines.d/`,
over ssh) and corroborated by the fleet convention's model-swap row. All four engines have served
Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_S.gguf since about 23:00Z on 2026-08-22 (gpu0, gpu1, gpu3) and about 23:20Z (gpu2);
each engine file now opens "SERVES Qwen3.6-35B-A3B since 2026-08-22". It was a decision, for roughly 4x decode
speed, not a fault. The id `zds-qwen3.8-27b` was KEPT as a historical alias: the router refuses any id absent
from its registry, so a caller that still names the retired id keeps working and is quietly handed the 35B
instead of failing loudly. The pod's real 27B lane existed from 2026-08-15 to 2026-08-22 only.

THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS LOG. Every batch recorded here ran on 2026-09-14 or 2026-09-15. That is a month after
the swap. So in thrusts 22 through 35, every arm launched with ZBENCH_POD_MODEL=zds-qwen3.8-27b was served by
Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, and so was every arm launched as zds-qwen3.5-35b: the two ids differ only in which engines
they pool, never in weights. Wherever this log says "the 27B", read "the 35B, through the 27B alias".

THE CAMPAIGN MEASURED THIS AND READ IT AS A RESULT. Thrust 22, "THE SMALLER-MODEL MAP", found the 27B matching
the 35B cell for cell -- 12/12 against 12/12, 12/12 against 12/12, 0/12 against 0/12 -- and wrote that cell
15's four misses "are all the microseconds isoformat default, the 35B's failure mode, no other". That is
exactly what one model compared with itself produces. The finding was not weak evidence of model similarity; it
was strong evidence of model IDENTITY, and it was read the other way.

WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT, stated plainly because it is fixable. The request dumps record what was ASKED, never
what answered: each wire-NNNN.json carries `model`, and a census of the surviving thrust 34 and 35 dumps reads
openai/zds-qwen3.8-27b in 909 of 909 calls across 120 run directories, with no other value present. No response
echo is stored anywhere in the bench, so no artifact of any arm in this campaign can say what served it. The pod's
`/v1/models` would have shown `canonical: zds-qwen3.6-35b` against every id on any day of the campaign, and
nothing read it.

WHAT SURVIVES, precisely. Every thrust here is an INTERLEAVED within-batch contrast: its baseline arms and its
candidate arms ran minutes apart on the same engines, against the same weights, whatever those weights were.
So the CONTRASTS stand as measured, and the ADR dispositions that rest on them stand. What does not stand is
every sentence that names a MODEL: the cross-model claims (thrust 22's map above all), and every statement of
the form "on the small model". These were measured on a 35B-A3B MoE, not on a 27B dense model.

WHAT THIS DOES TO THRUST 36. The design survives and the premise does not. The design is internally controlled:
a1 and a2 give 24 concurrent baseline runs against c1 and c2's 24 candidate runs, so it needs no historical
number. The premise was "a baseline WITH HEADROOM", and the 60% it names (50/84 untreated on cell 17, six
batches) is a 35B number. The real 27B's untreated rate on cell 17 is UNMEASURED. So is the sibling-read route
of about 23%. The predicted A of 11-17 in 24 rests on the 60% and is therefore withdrawn, along with the reason
cell 17 was chosen at all.

